\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Massless fields with a kernel-orthogonal constraint}
\date{2026-10-16}

\p{Let #{L} be self-adjoint positive semidefinite on a finite real
inner-product space #{(E,h)}, with the [kernel decomposition](fdip-0031)
#{K=\ker L}, #{H=K^\perp}, #{k=\dim K}, #{r=\dim H}.
The induced volume #{\lambda_H} gives the centered probability law
##{\begin{aligned}
Z_H&=\frac{(2\pi)^{r/2}}{\sqrt{\operatorname{pdet}L}},\\
d\nu_H(x)&=Z_H^{-1}e^{-h(x,Lx)/2}\,d\lambda_H(x).
\end{aligned}}
Its pushforward to #{E} has covariance #{L^+}. If #{k>0}, the
full-space massless integral is infinite, and the restriction of
ambient volume to #{H} is zero; neither replaces this induced measure.}
\proof{
\p{The operator #{L_H} is positive definite by \ref{fdip-0031}.
Apply [normalization](fdip-002T) and [inverse covariance](fdip-000B)
on the inner-product space #{H}. The induced measure is independent
of its orthonormal chart by [isometric volume preservation](fdip-002L).
For ambient sources #{j,k'\in E}, restriction of their linear
observables gives
##{\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Cov}_{i_*\nu_H}(\ell_j,\ell_{k'})
 &=h_H(p_Hj,L_H^{-1}p_Hk')\\
 &=h(j,L^+k'),
\end{aligned}}
using the [pseudoinverse construction](fdip-0032). Thus the ambient
covariance is singular when #{K\ne0}, although its restriction to
#{H} is an inverse.}
\p{Suppose #{k>0}. In an orthonormal chart #{H} is
#{\{0\}^k\times\mathbb R^r}. Each of its intersections with a finite
box has product volume zero; a countable union of these boxes covers
#{H}, proving zero ambient measure. For divergence, take a bounded
box of positive induced volume in #{H}. Continuity gives the weight
a strictly positive lower bound on its closure. On its product with
#{[-R,R]^k\subset K}, the weight is unchanged in the kernel direction,
so the integral is bounded below by a positive constant times
#{(2R)^k}. This tends to infinity. When #{H=0}, its singleton has
induced volume one, and the same argument applies.}
\p{When #{k=0}, #{H=E} and the full-space law already exists.
When #{E=0}, #{k=r=0}, both normalizers are one and the probability is
Dirac. When #{L=0} on a positive-dimensional space, #{H=0} gives the
constrained Dirac law while the full normalizer diverges. All determinant
products over zero-dimensional spaces are one.}
}
\p{For a connected nonempty graph with positive edge weights, put #{n=|V|}.
Then #{H} is exactly the
[mean-zero field space](fdip-0031), #{r=n-1}, and the formula becomes
#{Z_H=(2\pi)^{(n-1)/2}/\sqrt{\prod_{a=1}^{n-1}\lambda_a}}.
At #{n=1}, #{H=0}, #{Z_H=1}, and the full one-dimensional integral
still diverges. With #{k>1} components, a single global sum-zero
constraint removes only one dimension of the component-constant kernel:
the sum functional is nonzero on that #{k}-dimensional space, so its
kernel has dimension #{k-1}. The restricted integral still diverges
by the same kernel-box argument. Componentwise zero sums remove all
these modes. The empty graph uses the zero-dimensional case above.}
\p{[Massive factorization](fdip-0033) gives the exact divergent factor,
while [projected limits](fdip-0034) identify the finite limits of
polynomial and difference observables. [Vertex pinning](fdip-0035)
uses another constraint and generally another coordinate covariance,
as the [two-vertex example](fdip-0036) shows. The distinction between
free and fixed-boundary massless models also appears in
\citet{Definition 8.1, Remark 8.2 and Exercise 8.2, p. 381}{friedli2017statistical};
the induced-volume and finite-graph proofs here specify the present law.}
